<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eratosthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 276–c. 194 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Eratosthenes of Cyrene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eratosthenes of Cyrene was a Greek scholar who became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a> under the Ptolemies. He made a close estimate of the Earth's circumference from the angle of the sun at Syene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, drew a map of the known world with lines of latitude and longitude, and devised a method for finding prime numbers. His geography, quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and its peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eratosthenes.jpg" alt="Eratosthenes" width="278" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Eratosthenes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eratosthenes was the son of Aglaos and was born in Cyrene, a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in the second half of the 7th century BCE, which came under Ptolemaic rule after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. By the late 260s BCE he had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to study. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, he was taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a> there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, and he also studied under Arcesilaus of Pitane, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Platonic Academy</a>. He stayed in Athens for some twenty years, writing Platonikos, an inquiry into the mathematics and music in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s philosophy, poems, and works on chronology and on the winners of the Olympic Games.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 246 BCE Ptolemy III succeeded his father. The reigning librarian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, was forced into retirement, and Eratosthenes, known as a scholar and poet in the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>, was summoned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to replace him. The post included tutoring the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a>. Eratosthenes was closely associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>, who addressed his Method of Mechanical Theorems to him as a letter. According to the Suda, his sight failed in old age and he starved himself to death. His epitaph laments that he was buried in a foreign land and not at Cyrene.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known measurement is the circumference of the Earth. As Cleomedes reports it, at noon on the summer solstice the sun shone straight down a well at Syene, while at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> a vertical rod still cast a shadow of about 7.2 degrees, one fiftieth of a circle. Taking the distance between the cities as about 5,000 stadia, he multiplied by fifty to reach roughly 250,000 stadia. The method was sound, though two false assumptions, that Syene lay on the Tropic of Cancer and on the same meridian as Alexandria, happened to cancel each other out.</p><p>His three-volume Geography, which survives only in fragments, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, mapped the known world, divided the Earth into five climate zones and used parallels and meridians to place more than 400 cities. He is credited with coining the word geography. In his Chronographies he dated the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> to 1184/1183 BCE. The sieve of Eratosthenes, which finds prime numbers by marking off the multiples of each prime, carries his name, though one scholar holds that he named an older method. Admirers called him Pentathlos, the all-rounder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eratosthenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The incense trade carried Sabaean goods and reputation across the Near East and the Mediterranean, and Greek and Roman writers from <b>Eratosthenes</b>, quoted by Strabo, to Pliny the Elder made the Sabaeans a byword for the fabulous wealth of Arabia Felix.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was home to the Library of Alexandria and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as Euclid, <b>Eratosthenes</b> and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Eratosthenes</b>, in the 3rd century BCE, described the Noubai as a numerous people west of the Nile; Rilly links the word to Meroitic nob, used for the people and for “slave”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek chronographers, notably <b>Eratosthenes</b>, dated the fall of Troy to 1184 BCE, and later writers placed it in late April.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyrene, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-223" aria-label="Cyrene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Eratosthenes"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4416 4738h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4416 4738h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-223">What was here in 223 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
